            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Makes an array longer by putting values in at a position, moving everything after it along.\n\nUse it to add a joint to a skeleton's list, put a marker or a pause into a sequence, add a row to a table at a particular place. For adding at the end, np.append is simpler.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.insert <index: int>\nDefault: index 0 (at the start).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.insert -1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input has the values put in and is sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nvalues:\nWhat to put in, held: send it first. A single value, a list (put in together), or a row for a 2-D array.\n\nindex (int box, also an inlet):\nWhere to put them: 0 before the first element, 1 after it, and so on. -1 puts them at the very end, -2 before the last element. Beyond either end goes to that end.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way: 0 (the default) between elements of a list, or between rows of a matrix; 1 between columns.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ninserted:\nThe longer array.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n9 is put into 1 2 3 at index 1: 1 9 2 3. A metro re-sends the list twice a second; change the index to move the 9.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.append adds at the end. np.delete takes elements out. np.concatenate joins two arrays end to end. np.pad adds around the edges.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Makes an array longer by putting values in at a position, moving everything after it along.\n\nUse it to add a joint to a skeleton's list, put a marker or a pause into a sequence, add a row to a table at a particular place. For adding at the end, np.append is simpler.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.insert <index: int>\nDefault: index 0 (at the start).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.insert -1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input has the values put in and is sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nvalues:\nWhat to put in, held: send it first. A single value, a list (put in together), or a row for a 2-D array.\n\nindex (int box, also an inlet):\nWhere to put them: 0 before the first element, 1 after it, and so on. -1 puts them at the very end, -2 before the last element. Beyond either end goes to that end.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way: 0 (the default) between elements of a list, or between rows of a matrix; 1 between columns.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ninserted:\nThe longer array.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n9 is put into 1 2 3 at index 1: 1 9 2 3. A metro re-sends the list twice a second; change the index to move the 9.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.append adds at the end. np.delete takes elements out. np.concatenate joins two arrays end to end. np.pad adds around the edges."
        },
